/T (Generative models learn the underlying data distribution and generate new samples from it. They model the joint probability distribution of inputs and outputs, aiming to maximize the likelihood of the observed data. A common example is a language model, which predicts the next word in a sequence based on previous words.)
/E (Generative models learn the underlying data distribution and generate new samples from it. They model the joint probability distribution of inputs and outputs, aiming to maximize the likelihood of the observed data. A common example is a language model, which predicts the next word in a sequence based on previous words.)
